NAME
    rom - hash the library's games, check them, and find the same dump twice

SYNOPSIS
    rom hash [-r] [--comparable] <path>...
    rom check [-r] <path>...
    rom dups [<console>]
    rom wait [rom<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    The library's file tool of the emusen.library module. A <path> is a
    file or a folder of the library, AppSettings.RomDirectory, written
    relative to it with / between its parts: snes, "psx/Rayman (USA).cue".
    A folder is taken only with -r, and then whole, as the library's scan
    shelves it: a cue sheet stands for the tracks it names and an .m3u for
    its discs, and only files some console takes are read; / with -r is
    the whole library. A file named directly is read whatever it is.
    Which console a file is for is the scan's own routing, then the
    nearest console folder above it.

    THE LIBRARY IS ONLY READ. rom writes nothing anywhere: not into the
    library, not beside it. It reports; hiding a game or moving it to the
    Trash is the Library window's, and never this command's.

    'hash' prints one line for each file:

        <crc32> <md5> <sha1> <size> <name>

    the CRC-32 (as zip and ScreenScraper take it), MD5 and SHA-1 in lower-
    case hexadecimal, the size in bytes and the name relative to the
    library, one space between, so that the name is the rest of the line
    however many spaces it holds. A cue sheet gives one line for each
    track, as Redump lists a dump, named <sheet>#<nn>: the track's bytes
    from its first index in its file to the next track's. An .m3u gives
    no line of its own; its discs follow it. A zip is hashed as the file
    it is.

    With --comparable each file gives one line, of its console's
    comparable form, the form in which two copies of one dump are equal:
      - a cartridge with the header its console's pack takes off removed
        (the iNES header of an NES game, a SNES copier's 512 bytes, an N64
        image put in big-endian order); otherwise the file as it is;
      - a zip as the image inside it that a game is played from, checked
        against its CRC-32 and then taken as above;
      - a disc (a cue sheet and its tracks, a CHD, an .iso, a raw data
        track) as EmuSen's own disc reader gives its sectors: a compact
        disc's 2,352 raw bytes each from track 1's index 01 to the lead-
        out, a DVD's 2,048 user bytes each, its size the disc's. So a cue
        sheet of one file, the same disc split into a file a track, and
        its CHD give one line.

    'check' prints one line for each file: '<name>: ok', or the name and
    each check it failed, as '<check>: <why>', separated by '; '. The checks
    are those a dump can be held to without a catalogue of good dumps:
        size     the file will not open, will not read through, or is
                 empty; an .iso or raw track that is not a whole number of
                 its 2,048- or 2,352-byte sectors;
        sheet    a cue sheet that does not parse, names a file that is not
                 there, has a track that ends inside a sector, or does not
                 make a disc; an .m3u listing a disc that is not there;
        archive  a zip whose directory does not read, which holds no file,
                 or an entry whose bytes do not match the length and CRC-32
                 the zip records for it;
        chd      a CHD that chdman's verify fails (every hunk decompressed,
                 the overall SHA-1 compared), or one of whose sectors
                 EmuSen's reader cannot read (a hunk whose CRC is not the
                 map's). Where the build's chdman is not there, or is not
                 the pinned one, rom check says so once and reads each CHD
                 through EmuSen's reader alone.
    A header that does not hold its own checksum is a later check, by each
    console's pack. A clean check is not a proof that the dump is a good
    one: only a catalogue of good dumps can say that.

    'dups' prints the groups of the same dump, one a paragraph, each file
    as its 'hash --comparable' line: files whose comparable CRC-32, MD5,
    SHA-1 and size are all equal. A package and the library files it
    claims are one game already (a CHD the tool wrote beside its original,
    and the original), so a group of those alone is no group. With a
    <console>, by its ES-DE name or its id (snes, psx), only that console's
    games are compared. A file that cannot be read is named on standard
    error and left out; no group is given if the job was stopped before
    every file was read. The same pairs come from the shell's own tools:

        rom hash -r --comparable / | sort | uniq -D -w 40

    since the first 40 characters of a line are the CRC-32 and most of the
    MD5; dups adds the paragraphs and the packages' rule.

    Each rom is a job of the library tool, followed until it ends or the
    line's ten seconds are spent; a longer one goes on in the background as
    rom<n>, which ps lists with how many files it has read, 'rom wait
    rom<n>' follows again, printing what it found meanwhile, and 'kill
    rom<n>' stops. What rom prints on standard output is only its lines,
    so that it pipes; its notes and the words for a file it could not read
    go to standard error. A pipe is bound by the same ten seconds, so a
    whole library is run as 'rom dups' and waited for.

    Exit status: 1 where a path names nothing, a file could not be read or
    failed a check; otherwise 0. dups finding groups is not a failure.

EXAMPLES
    rom hash "snes/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    rom hash psx/Rayman.cue
    rom hash -r --comparable snes | sort
    rom check -r / | grep -v ': ok$'
    rom dups
    rom dups psx
    rom hash -r --comparable / | sort | uniq -D -w 40

